    "q-269470": {
      "dom_id": "step-q269470",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-2b)^{T}(Ax-2b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$",
      "worked_solution": "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-2b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-2b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-2b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
