    "q-269518": {
      "dom_id": "step-q269518",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=2(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$",
      "worked_solution": "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=2{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{T}y)=4{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 16 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
